          "content": "The laundromat on Alder Street had a shelf labeled TAKE A BOOK, LEAVE A BOOK. For three years it held a waterlogged atlas, two detective novels, and a manual for a dishwasher nobody owned. Then someone left a notebook.\n\nOn the first page, in pencil: *What did you almost do today?*\n\nBy Thursday the answers had begun. A man had almost bought a pear but remembered the pears at home. Someone had almost called her brother. Someone else had almost stolen the notebook, which seemed fair enough, given the sign.\n\nOver the winter the notebook filled. I read it while my clothes spun. The brother appeared again in February: *I called. He wanted to know how to cook rice.* Below that, in different handwriting, were instructions for cooking rice.\n\nOne evening the dryers stopped halfway through everybody\u2019s loads. We stood around holding warm, damp sleeves as the owner went downstairs to check the breaker. A woman at the folding table opened the notebook. She read aloud the entry about the pear.\n\n\u201cThat was me,\u201d said the man beside her.\n\nShe looked up. \u201cWere they any good? The ones at home?\u201d\n\n\u201cNo,\u201d he said. \u201cThey made that noise when you bit into them. Like stepping in snow.\u201d\n\nThe lights came back. Nobody moved for a second. Then the woman reached into her shopping bag, took out a pear, and cut it in half with a little folding knife. The man ate his half over the laundry sink so it wouldn\u2019t drip.",
